  "step_1": "The decision reason evaluates the probable outcomes based on a threshold: an outcome is considered probable if its probability is greater than 1 divided by the number of outcomes. There are two outcomes in each lottery (win or lose), so the threshold is 1/2 = 0.5. Thus, any outcome with probability > 0.5 is considered probable. In Lottery A, both outcomes (3000 Euros with 50% probability and -3000 Euros with 50% probability) have probability exactly 0.5, which is not greater than 0.5. Therefore, neither outcome is classified as probable. In Lottery B, there is one outcome (2000 Euros with 100% probability), which has probability 1.0 > 0.5, so it is considered probable. Thus, Lottery B has one probable outcome, while Lottery A has zero. The reason prefers the lottery with a higher average of probable outcomes — here, Lottery B has one probable outcome, A has zero — so the reason would prefer Lottery B.",
